Z||000006||Slammed Fiero||06-20-2001||01:18 PM||||I got mine from groung control in the states. $199 , took 1 month to ship them, as they don't readily stock them<BR> <A HREF="http://www.ground-control.com" TARGET=_blank>www.ground-control.com</A> <P>I just checked , they still offer them for the rear of the fiero. Keep in mind the coil overs for the fiero , will be for the rear only.<P>I have installed a set of used fiero struts on a V6 cavalier before. So logically if you get them for a V6 cav , they should fit the back of the fiero. Usually the sleeve itself has to be notched to sit propery on the strut, or <P>I had to go with coilovers due to 255's in the rear , the strut collar was dangerously close to the sidewall.<P>IF you do decide to go with coilovers and modify the strut collar , remove the whole strut and put it in the freezer for an hour , then grind the collar off and use a hose to cool the strut body. PErhaps this is extreme , but gas expands and you don't want a strut to blow up , I have seen it , it's scary and schrapnel flys everywhere.<P>what is your goal with coilovers , whats the purpose of the car?? if you just wanna look good and handle better , I would go with a complete set of springs. Fierolisa has some really nice suspension techniques springs on her car. She got them for under $200 if My memory serves me correctly.<P>I had the Fierostore springs and a set of eibach springs. Both good , but I found for the money  discount tire direct sells the cheapest way to lower a Fiero (suspension techniques)<P>Where is Weyburn??<BR>I was Born in Yorkton....<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">      <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals. &lt;b&gt; New White Face Gauges , 75Hp Shot of NOS&lt;/b&gt;Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000007||underdog||06-20-2001||02:42 PM||||weyburn is 1 hour south east of regina, in the area of estevan....the south east corner.<P>cool to hear of someone so close, as i doubt you still ive in yorkton though hey<P><BR>i want to lower my car, and do it for a reasonable price. the fiero store has the springs for like 250 american and that seems a little high. all i want is the lowering effect, that is all, just for looks. what can you suggest as being the best way, and if possible point me in the direction of the product<P>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000008||Slammed Fiero||06-20-2001||03:01 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/adjustable_rear_strut.jpg"> <P><BR>this is a coilover setup on a koni strut.<P>Coilovers are not a cheap way to lower a car.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.discounttiredirect.com" TARGET=_blank>www.discounttiredirect.com</A> <P>$188 for 4 springs..about $300 after you get them in Canada. There the suspension tecnniques units.<P>I would use these for your purposes.<P>No I live in Kingston Ontario / Barrie now. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Hope this helps , you may wanna keep your eyes out for a used set. I sold my fiero store lowering springs for $175 Canadian.<P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">      <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals. &lt;b&gt; New White Face Gauges , 75Hp Shot of NOS&lt;/b&gt;Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
